# Vauxhall 14-6 four-door saloon, 1939-1948.
#
# The 14-6: announced in October 1938 and built from 1939, with a unitary
# hull rather than the Light Six separate chassis, a 1,781 cc six and a
# three-speed all-synchromesh gearbox. The four-door six-light saloon is
# modelled; the Australian separate-chassis bodies are not represented.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vauxhall_14-6
#
# Dimensions: wheelbase 105 in (2.667 m), length 168 in (4.267 m), width
# 63.5 in (1.613 m). Height was not established from a source and is
# estimated from the photograph.
#
# Shape reference: NO true level profile exists. Image 1 is about 20 degrees
# behind the side plane, camera slightly above waist height, so the flank is
# estimated within the published dimensions. No rear three-quarter of a UK
# 14-6 was found, so image 3 is a 1947 front quarter in its place.
#
# Colours: grade (d); no Vauxhall colour list was found. Black, Maroon, Cream
# and Dark Blue are colours observed on surviving cars. Weights 12, 10, 9, 8,
# total 39, provisional.
#
# Speed 110 km/h. Comfort 30/375, on the line 330 + 1.5 x 30. Classes 1,2.
# Dates: intro January 1939; retires staggered 1957-1960 as a road tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/vauxhall-14-6.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/vauxhall-14-6.dae. References:
# citycars/references/vauxhall-14-6/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: the post-war UK grille and bonnet trim represent the
# range, not the 1939 trim. The tail, the boot contour and the split rear
# glazing are inferred from an oblique side view, there being no dedicated rear
# reference; auxiliary lamps and overriders are omitted.


